  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins and Structure
    
    A trade charter forged during the Clever Ice Age — the era of guild-schools and fire-core innovation — in The Drowned Ledge, a Nightshelf mining outpost since lost to catastrophic flooding. Pactum∴ne was written in ash and sealed with the prints of Night-Assessor Cevmucsiatum-larv~ and ☽'tenebris, granting the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, a Nightshelf merchant guild, exclusive passage through the Drowned Ledge's trade routes in exchange for guaranteed fire-core and aurora-crystal shipments to the surface. The charter bound signatories to specific volumes; reduction triggered renegotiation — and renegotiation meant risking the Drowned Ledge passage itself, the sole route keeping fire-core flowing outward and krill-stock flowing in.
    
    Pactum∴ne subsumed Lex∴andatum, the Nightshelf's unified legal code, within its operational scope, and contained emergency provisions authorizing supply interdiction without formal denial-of-trade registration with the Shadow Parliament. The ☽'ofund' Guild invoked these provisions during the Faction Wars to interdict supply to the Midnight Claws, a Nightshelf criminal faction. The resulting records are incomplete. The action was never formally filed.
    
    ## Enshrined Artifacts
    
    Two artifacts were bound within the charter's authority: **☽'visois' Mask**, carried by enforcers such as the chronicled ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer, and **Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~**, borne by couriers on routes abandoned after the Mbra∴silentiu — a contested-waters conflagration — burned the lower passages. A clause requiring enshrined weapons to pass by guild law and assessor judgment was never enforced before the charter's authority collapsed.
    
    ## Crisis and Collapse
    
    Following the Profun∴lapsum, a thermal rupture that devastated Nightshelf fire-mines and killed dozens, the charter governed terms under which the Nightshelf traded three years of crystal-debt to Aurora Stack for food — its provisions enforced even as capacity to honor original commitments collapsed around it. The Frozen Peace and successive wars finished what the Profun∴lapsum began.
    
    The parchment — if it survives — is kept in a vault beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot, a deep-tunnel merchant collective. No one remembers when it was last checked. The **Zen∴ctum**, once a celebration of the charter's renewal, is now a funeral rite observed in diminished darkness. What Pactum∴ne constrains today is not trade but pride.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771588183207_0", anchor: "killed dozens", text: "Thermal-impression density in the relevant strata is consistent with hundreds present in the affected tunnels. 'Dozens' is the number someone chose to commit to writing. It is not the number the ice recorded.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588183207_1", anchor: "subsumed Lex∴andatum", text: "'Within its operational scope' is performing structural work this sentence cannot survive without. A trade charter claiming authority over the unified legal code of an entire civilization is not a routine jurisdictional detail. The Lex∴andatum entry treats the code as sovereign. This entry treats it as subordinate. Both hold only if that qualifier stands — and the qualifier was contested by every Shadow Parliament session for which records survive. Three ended in violence. I wrote both entries. They disagree with each other. I have left the disagreement because the sources disagree, and pretending otherwise would be a worse kind of error.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588183207_2", anchor: "No one remembers when it was last checked", text: "A charter sealed in ash, kept in a vault no one opens, beneath a collective whose trade routes no longer function, in a civilization the Council is preparing to call something other than what it is. I catalogue things like this. That is the whole of my profession — ensuring that when someone finally opens the vault and finds the parchment gone, or crumbled, or still miraculously whole, there will be a record that it mattered. Assuming anyone checks the record.", type: :commentary, display: :full
